ca set name is user-service-cdd678d87, so the pod name would be something like user-service-cdd678d87-*. So perhaps I should get the pod name first. But the user\'s previous command was "kubectl describe pod -n social-network", which might not be specific. Maybe the correct approach is to first get the list of pods with the service label, then describe the specific one. However, the user might not have the exact pod name. Alternatively, using "kubectl describe pod -n social-network" without a name would list all pods, but that\'s not efficient. Wait, the user\'s last command was "kubectl describe pod -n social-network", but that\'s ambiguous. The correct command should target the specific pod. But since the user doesn\'t have the pod name, maybe they need to first list the pods. So perhaps the next step is to run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to find the pod name related to user-service. Then, use that name to describe the pod. But the user\'s previous attempt was to describe the pod without specifying the name, which might not work. So the correct tool call here would be to first get the list of pods in the social-network namespace with the label "service=user-service" to identify the specific pod name. Then, describe that pod to get detailed information. However, the user\'s available tools include "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", so the next step is to run "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to find the pod name. Alternatively, maybe the user can use "kubectl describe pod <pod-name>" once the name is known. But since the user hasn\'t specified the pod name yet, the next logical step is to list the pods to find the relevant one. Therefore, the correct tool call is "kubectl get pods -n social-network" to identify the pod name associated with the user-service deployment.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-837