let\'s see. The user-service pod is pending because of scheduling issues. The event says there\'s a node affinity conflict and nodes don\'t match the pod\'s selectors. The node selector in the pod spec is set to "kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net". So, maybe the node with that hostname isn\'t available or there\'s a conflict with the volume.\n\nFirst, I should check if the node specified in the node selector exists. If the node is down or not reachable, the pod can\'t schedule. Also, the volume node affinity conflict might be related to the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) named "temp-pvc". I need to check if that PVC is bound and if the associated PersistentVolume (PV) is correctly set up with the right node affinity.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the PVC details. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to get info about the PVC. If the PVC isn\'t bound, that\'s a problem. Also, looking at the PV\'s spec to see if it has node affinity that conflicts with the pod\'s node selector. If the PV is bound to a different node or has restrictions, that could cause the scheduling issue. Let\'s check the PVC and PV details to confirm.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pvc temp-pvc -n social-network"  \nTo investigate the volume node affinity conflict, I need to check the status and details of the PVC `temp-pvc` associated with the user-service pod. This will reveal whether the PVC is bound to a PersistentVolume (PV), and if there are any node affinity constraints or misconfigurations preventing the pod from scheduling on the required node (`node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net`). This is critical to resolve the scheduling conflict.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 391, 'prompt_tokens': 5071, 'total_tokens': 5462, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Q