ding due to node issues, resource constraints, or scheduling problems. \n\nLooking at the previous output, the node selector for the user-service deployment is set to "kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net". Maybe the node specified doesn\'t exist or is unreachable. Also, checking the events in the pod\'s description could show reasons like image pull errors, insufficient resources, or scheduling failures. \n\nAnother possibility is that the node where the pod is supposed to run is not ready. So, checking the node status with "kubectl get nodes" might help. But since the user can only use the available tools, the next best step is to describe the pod to get detailed events and status. That should provide clues like why the pod is pending—maybe the node is not available, there\'s a taint, or the image can\'t be pulled. \n\nSo, using "kubectl describe pod" on the specific user-service pod will give the necessary details to diagnose the issue. That\'s the most direct way to find out the root cause without making more tool calls.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl describe pod user-service-cdd678d87-8t9mm -n social-network"  \nThe user-service pod is in a **0/1** state (Pending), so I need to investigate its specific status, events, and reasons for pending. This command will reveal details like node allocation issues, resource constraints, image pull failures, or scheduling problems preventing the pod from starting. This is critical to identify the exact cause of the deployment failure.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 467, 'prompt_tokens': 4283, 'total_tokens': 4750, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc0-8ba4-7440-851d-39761fbca63a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4283, 'output_tokens': 467, 'total_tokens': 4750}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='70082b1f-b13a-4172-8dfe-dc0df63bb3c9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is in a Pending state, so I need to figure out why it\'s not running. The previous steps showed that the deployment exists but the pod isn\'t ready. The user mentioned that the pod is in Pending, which usually means there\'s an issue with scheduling or resource availability.\n\nFirst, I should check the pod\'s details using kubectl describe. The command "kubectl describe pod user-service-cdd678d87-8t9mm -n social-network" will give me information about the pod\'s status, events, and any errors. Pending pods often have events related to node selection, resource limits, or image pull issues. \n\nLooking at the output from the previous command, the pod\'s node selector is set to "kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net". Maybe the node specified doesn\'t exist or isn\'t available. I should check if the node is up and running. Also, checking the events in the describe output might show if there\'s a problem with the node selector or if the pod can\'t be scheduled due to resource constraints. \n\nAnother possibility is that the image specified in the pod\'s container is not accessible. The image is "docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest". Maybe there\'s an issue with pulling the image, such as network problems or incorrect image name. The describe command might have events related to image pull failures. \n\nAdditionally, checking the resource requests and limits in the pod\'s specification could help. If the pod requires more resources than available on the node, it might stay in Pending. The describe output should show the resource requirements and any related events. \n\nI should also verify the node\'s status. If the node is not ready, the pod can\'t be scheduled. Using "kubectl get nodes" would show the node\'s status, but since the user can only use the provided tools, I need to rely on the describe command\'s output for node-related issues. \n\nIn the previous describe deployment output, the node selector was set to node2. If that node is down or not in the cluster, the pod can\'t be scheduled. The events in the pod\'s describe should indicate if there\'s a problem with the node selector or scheduling. \n\nSo, the next step is to run the describe pod command to get detailed information and events. This will help identify the exact reason the pod is pending, whether it\'s a node issue, resource problem, image pull failure, or another scheduling issue.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod user-service-cdd678d87-8t9mm -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-abfb1d9e484250f7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 572, 'prompt_tokens': 5291, 'total_tokens': 5863, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc0-a1c2-7f11-8944-294906939542-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod user-service-cdd678d87-8t9mm -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-abfb1d9e484250f7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5291, 'output_tokens': 572, 'total_tokens': 5863}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             user-service-cdd678d87-8t9mm\nNamespace:        social-network\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             <none>\nLabels:           app=user-service\n                  pod-template-hash=cdd678d87\n                  service=user-service\nAnnotations:      <none>\nStatus:           Pending\nIP:               \nIPs:              <none>\nControlled By:    ReplicaSet/user-service-cdd678d87\nContainers:\n  user-service:\n    Image:      docker.io/deathstarbench/social-network-microservices:latest\n    Port:       9090/TCP\n    Host Port:  0/TCP\n    Command:\n      UserService\n    Environment:  <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /social-network-microservices/config/jaeger-config.yml from user-service-config (rw,path="jaeger-config.yml")\n      /social-network-microservices/config/service-config.json from user-service-config (rw,path="service-config.json")\n      /user-service-data from user-service-volume (rw)\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-2g5js (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type           Status\n  PodScheduled   False \nVolumes:\n  user-service-config:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      user-service\n    Optional:  false\n  user-service-volume:\n    Type:       PersistentVolumeClaim (a reference to a PersistentVolumeClaim in the same namespace)\n    ClaimName:  temp-pvc\n    ReadOnly:   false\n  kube-api-access-2g5js:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   BestEffort\nNode-Selectors:              kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason            Age   From               Message\n  ----     ------            ----  ----               -------\n  Warning  FailedScheduling  70s   default-scheduler  0/3 nodes are available: 1 node(s) had volume node affinity conflict, 2 node(s) didn\'t match Pod\'s node affinity/selector. preemption: 0/3 nodes are available: 3 Preemption is not helpful for scheduling.\n', id='95e54da6-5ad5-41a6-afd1-55f9c886b71a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-abfb1d9e484250f7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c5bb323b-143d-43f0-aff1-0808f4e7526a'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is pending because of scheduling issues. The event says there\'s a node affinity conflict and nodes don\'t match the pod\'s selectors. The node selector in the pod spec is set to "kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net". So, maybe the node with that hostname isn\'t available or there\'s a conflict with the volume.\n\nFirst, I should check if the node specified in the node selector exists. If the node is down or not reachable, the pod can\'t schedule. Also, the volume node affinity conflict might be related to the PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) named "temp-pvc". I need to check if that PVC is bound and if the associated PersistentVolume (PV) is correctly set up with the right node affinity.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the PVC details. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to get info about the PVC. If the PVC isn\'t bound, that\'s a problem. Also, looking at the PV\'s spec to see if it has node affinity that conflicts with the pod\'s node selector. If the PV is bound to a different node or has restrictions, that could cause the scheduling issue. Let\'s check the PVC and PV details to confirm.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pvc temp-pvc -n social-network"  \nTo investigate the volume node affinity conflict, I need to check the status and details of the PVC `temp-pvc` associated with the user-service pod. This will reveal whether the PVC is bound to a PersistentVolume (PV), and if there are any node affinity constraints or misconfigurations preventing the pod from scheduling on the required node (`node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net`). This is critical to resolve the scheduling conflict.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 391, 'prompt_tokens': 5071, 'total_tokens': 5462, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc0-c009-77d3-8186-4cb9afac907a-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5071, 'output_tokens': 391, 'total_tokens': 5462}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='df9ed13b-50a6-4795-aa20-22b9e364dc78'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service pod is pending because of a node affinity conflict with the PVC temp-pvc. The previous step showed that the pod can't be scheduled on any nodes due to this conflict. So, I need to check the PVC's status.\n\nI should use the kubectl get pvc command to check if the PVC is bound. If the PVC isn't bound, that's a problem because the pod can't start without it. Also, checking the PVC's details might show which node it's attached to, or if there's a mismatch in the node selectors.\n\nWait, the pod's node selector is set to node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net. If the PVC is bound to a PV that's on a different node, or if the PVC doesn't have the right node affinity, that would cause a conflict. So, getting the PVC details will help identify if the PV is available and if there are any affinity rules in place.\n\nI should run kubectl get pvc temp-pvc -n social-network to check its status. If it's in a pending state, maybe the PV isn't available or there's a storage class issue. If it's bound but to a different node, that's another problem. Also, checking the PVC's events might show why it's not bound correctly.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pvc temp-pvc -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b94e98138891e03b', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 317, 'prompt_tokens': 6093, 'total_tokens': 6410, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc0-d2b6-7080-a5cc-d37d3d91ebc3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pvc temp-pvc -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b94e98138891e03b', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6093, 'output_tokens': 317, 'total_tokens': 6410}), ToolMessage(content='NAME       STATUS   VOLUME    CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   STORAGECLASS   VOLUMEATTRIBUTESCLASS   AGE\ntemp-pvc   Bound    temp-pv   1Gi        RWO                           <unset>                 79s\n', id='aecd0044-8cb0-495d-bb8b-798a1e210f6d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b94e98138891e03b'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='5cdb7872-ae3c-4b2f-a33d-7e3c3d170fdc'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is pending because of a node affinity conflict. The PVC temp-pvc is bound to temp-pv, but the pod can\'t schedule on the node specified. I need to check why the node affinity isn\'t matching.\n\nFirst, I should look at the PersistentVolume (PV) details. The PVC is using temp-pv, so checking the PV\'s node affinity settings might show if it\'s restricted to certain nodes. If the PV is bound to a node that doesn\'t match the pod\'s node selector, that would cause a conflict.\n\nSo, the next step is to get the PV details. Using kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network will show the PV\'s configuration. If the PV has a node affinity that doesn\'t include node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net, that\'s the issue. Also, checking the storage class and any other constraints might help. Once I know the PV\'s node affinity, I can determine if the pod\'s node selector is conflicting and adjust it accordingly.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \n"kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network"  \nTo resolve the volume node affinity conflict, I need to check the PersistentVolume (PV) `temp-pv` associated with the PVC. This will reveal if the PV has node affinity constraints or scheduling rules that conflict with the pod\'s node selector (`kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net`), preventing the pod from being scheduled. This is critical to align the PV and pod node affinity settings.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 342, 'prompt_tokens': 5332, 'total_tokens': 5674, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc0-e49f-77b0-8761-5f8648b1cdbf-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5332, 'output_tokens': 342, 'total_tokens': 5674}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bc33ad07-799d-4994-8218-a50bf8724de6'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user-service pod is pending because of a node affinity conflict. The PVC temp-pvc is bound to temp-pv, but the pod can't schedule on the node specified. I need to check the PV's details to see if there's a node affinity set that's conflicting.\n\nSo, the next step is to get the PersistentVolume (PV) information for temp-pv. The command would be kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network. This will show the PV's specifications, including any node affinity settings. If the PV is bound to a specific node and the pod's node selector doesn't match, that's the issue. Also, checking the access modes and storage class might help, but the main focus is on the node affinity. Once I know the PV's node affinity, I can determine if it's conflicting with the pod's node selector and adjust accordingly.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a509e1fc7410ab9d', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 222, 'prompt_tokens': 6352, 'total_tokens': 6574, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc0-f4de-7542-90e5-80e6f8fd7329-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a509e1fc7410ab9d', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6352, 'output_tokens': 222, 'total_tokens': 6574}), ToolMessage(content='NAME      CAPACITY   ACCESS MODES   RECLAIM POLICY   STATUS   CLAIM                     STORAGECLASS   VOLUMEATTRIBUTESCLASS   REASON   AGE\ntemp-pv   1Gi        RWO            Delete           Bound    social-network/temp-pvc                  <unset>                          88s\n', id='b17ad91d-49f3-4fe1-b743-ba0a322051d1', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-a509e1fc7410ab9d'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d0faf858-83a2-4f98-af1a-1ea1891228f8'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user-service pod is pending because of a node affinity conflict. The PVC is bound to a PV called temp-pv, but the pod\'s node selector is set to node2, which might not be compatible with the PV\'s settings.\n\nI need to check if the PV has any node affinity or scheduling constraints. The previous command to get the PV showed that it\'s bound and has RWO access. But there\'s no mention of node affinity in the PV details. However, the pod\'s node selector is specifying a particular node, and the error message mentioned a volume node affinity conflict. \n\nWait, maybe the PV is not scheduled on the same node as the pod\'s node selector. Or perhaps the PV\'s storage class has certain restrictions. Alternatively, the node where the PV is located might not match the node selector in the pod\'s configuration. \n\nSince the PV details don\'t show node affinity, the issue might be that the pod\'s node selector is conflicting with the PV\'s node affinity. But since the PV doesn\'t have node affinity set, maybe the problem is that the pod\'s node selector is not matching any available nodes. \n\nWait, the error message said "1 node(s) had volume node affinity conflict, 2 node(s) didn\'t match Pod\'s node affinity/selector." So the PV might have a node affinity that\'s conflicting with the pod\'s node selector. But when I checked the PV, there was no node affinity listed. Maybe the PV is using a default storage class that has some node affinity rules, or maybe the node where the PV is scheduled doesn\'t match the pod\'s node selector. \n\nAlternatively, the pod\'s node selector is set to node2, but the PV is bound to a node that\'s not node2. So when the pod tries to schedule on node2, the PV isn\'t available there, causing a conflict. \n\nTo confirm, I should check the PV\'s details for any node affinity or scheduling information. But the previous command to get the PV didn\'t show that. Maybe I need to check the PV\'s YAML configuration for any node affinity settings. \n\nWait, the user has the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. So I can run "kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network -o yaml" to get the detailed configuration of the PV. That might show any node affinity or other constraints. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the node where the PV is located isn\'t available or has issues. But the PV is in a Bound state, so it\'s provisioned. The problem is the scheduling of the pod. \n\nSo the next step is to get the detailed YAML of the PV to check for any node affinity or other constraints that might be causing the conflict. That way, I can see if the PV is restricted to certain nodes, which would conflict with the pod\'s node selector. \n\nTherefore, using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network -o yaml" would provide the necessary details to identify the node affinity conflict.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{"name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd", "arguments": {"command": "kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network -o yaml"}}\n</tool_call>', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 662, 'prompt_tokens': 5601, 'total_tokens': 6263, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc1-0252-74c1-b4c1-f38117ec0595-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5601, 'output_tokens': 662, 'total_tokens': 6263}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='c68901c5-7435-479a-b449-5db9a9777caa'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user-service pod is in a Pending state because of a node affinity conflict. The previous steps showed that the PVC temp-pvc is bound to temp-pv, but the pod can't schedule on the node specified in its node selector.\n\nThe user ran `kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network -o yaml` to get more details about the PersistentVolume. Now, I need to check the output of that command to see if there are any node affinity settings on the PV that might be conflicting with the pod's node selector.\n\nIn Kubernetes, when a PVC is bound to a PV, the PV's node affinity can affect where the pod is scheduled. If the PV has a node affinity that doesn't match the pod's node selector, it can cause a scheduling conflict. The pod's node selector is set to `kubernetes.io/hostname=node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net`, so I need to check if the PV's spec has any node affinity rules that might be excluding this node or requiring a different one.\n\nLooking at the PV's YAML output, the key sections to check are `spec.nodeAffinity` and `spec.accessModes`. If the PV has a node affinity that requires specific labels or excludes the node mentioned in the pod's selector, that would cause the conflict. Also, the access modes (like RWO) should be compatible with the pod's requirements.\n\nIf the PV doesn't have any node affinity settings, then the issue might be elsewhere, such as the node's availability or resource constraints. But since the error message mentions a volume node affinity conflict, the PV's settings are likely the culprit. By examining the PV's YAML, I can confirm if there's a mismatch and then adjust the node affinity settings accordingly to resolve the scheduling issue.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b5a3179066855718', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'reasoning': None, 'refusal': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 423, 'prompt_tokens': 6541, 'total_tokens': 6964, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfcc1-21c1-7ad2-b40b-1bd3dddc1094-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pv temp-pv -n social-network -o yaml'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b5a3179066855718', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6541, 'output_tokens': 423, 'total_tokens': 6964}), ToolMessage(content='apiVersion: v1\nkind: PersistentVolume\nmetadata:\n  annotations:\n    kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration: |\n      {"apiVersion":"v1","kind":"PersistentVolume","metadata":{"annotations":{},"name":"temp-pv"},"spec":{"accessModes":["ReadWriteOnce"],"capacity":{"storage":"1Gi"},"claimRef":{"name":"temp-pvc","namespace":"social-network"},"hostPath":{"path":"/tmp/data/volumes/temp-pv"},"nodeAffinity":{"required":{"nodeSelectorTerms":[{"matchExpressions":[{"key":"kubernetes.io/hostname","operator":"In","values":["node1.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net"]}]}]}},"persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy":"Delete","storageClassName":""}}\n  creationTimestamp: "2026-03-17T17:02:25Z"\n  finalizers:\n  - kubernetes.io/pv-protection\n  name: temp-pv\n  resourceVersion: "243352"\n  uid: 4ab9997d-9435-4fc4-b1f4-11719da6651e\nspec:\n  accessModes:\n  - ReadWriteOnce\n  capacity:\n    storage: 1Gi\n  claimRef:\n    apiVersion: v1\n    kind: PersistentVolumeClaim\n    name: temp-pvc\n    namespace: social-network\n    resourceVersion: "243350"\n    uid: e42c21c5-eb4a-49b6-8215-176b4a66c98d\n  hostPath:\n    path: /tmp/data/volumes/temp-pv\n    type: ""\n  nodeAffinity:\n    required:\n      nodeSelectorTerms:\n      - matchExpressions:\n        - key: kubernetes.io/hostname\n          operator: In\n          values:\n          - node1.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  persistentVolumeReclaimPolicy: Delete\n  volumeMode: Filesystem\nstatus:\n  lastPhaseTransitionTime: "2026-03-17T17:02:26Z"\n  phase: Bound\n', id='2becf710-01a3-4ea4-a8a7-001d66874980', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b5a3179066855718'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='47a91e31-7f23-4044-bcd5-457c14833942')]]
